(Amended) 

The Standing Committee of the National Assembly has the following rights and duties: 

1.  Prepare and convene sessions of the National Assembly; 

2.  Propose the drafting, amendment, interpretation, and explanation of the Constitution and laws; 

3.  Propose draft legislation to the President of the State. 

4.  Appoint, transfer, or remove the heads of the court panels of the Supreme People’s Court and 

judges of the People’s Court; 

5.  Appoint the members of the Supreme People’s Prosecutor Committee; 

6.  Appoint the members of the National Election Committee; 

7.  Consider and decide on the ratification, accession, or withdrawal of international treaties to which 

the Lao PDR is a party; 

8.  Receive and consider petitions submitted by citizens seeking justice.

(Amended) 

The following entities have the right to propose draft laws: 

13 




 1.  The President of the State; 

2.  The Standing Committee of the National Assembly; 

3.  The Government; 

4.  The Supreme People’s Court; 

5.  The Supreme People’s Prosecutor; 

6.  The State Inspection Authority; 

7.  The State Audit Organization; 

8.  The Lao Front for National Construction, the Lao Veterans Federation, and mass organizations at 

the central level.

(Amended) 

The President has the following rights and responsibilities: 

1.  To promulgate the Constitution and laws; 

2.  To issue presidential ordinances and decrees; 

3.  To convene and preside over special meetings of the Government, and to participate in Standing 

Committee meetings of the National Assembly when necessary; 

15 


 4.  To appoint, transfer, or dismiss the Deputy Prime Minister and members of the Government, after 

approval by the National Assembly; 

5.  To appoint, transfer, or dismiss the Vice President of the Supreme People’s Court, the Vice 

President of the Supreme People’s Prosecutor Office, the Vice President of the State Inspection 
Authority, the Deputy Head of the Central Anti-Corruption Authority, and the Vice President of 
the State Audit Organization; 

6.  To decide on the promotion, demotion or removal of military ranks within the national defense 

and security forces; 

7.  To appoint, transfer, or dismiss the Head and Deputy Head of the Office of the President; 

8.  To appoint or recall Ambassadors Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of the Lao PDR abroad, and 

to accept or reject foreign Ambassadors or Permanent Representatives accredited to Laos; 

9.  To declare the formation of the national election commission, election dates, number of 

candidates, and seats for the National Assembly and provincial People’s Assemblies, based on 
proposals from the Standing Committee of the National Assembly; 

10.  To declare war or peace based on the decision of the National Assembly; to approve general or 

partial mobilization and the state of emergency nationwide or in specific regions; 

11.  To award or revoke national decorations such as Orders of Merit, Medals of Honor, Victory 

Medals, or other honorary titles; 

12.  To grant pardons and declare amnesties, after approval by the National Assembly; 

13.  To grant political asylum to foreign individuals. 

14.  To decide on negotiations or signings, to declare ratification or accession, and to approve or 

terminate treaties in the name of the Lao People’s Democratic Republic.

(Amended) 

The Government has the following rights and duties: 

1.  To implement the strategic directions, Constitution, laws, resolutions of the National Assembly 
and of the Standing Committee of the National Assembly, as well as presidential ordinances and 
decrees; 

2.  To submit draft laws to the National Assembly, draft presidential ordinances to the Standing 

Committee of the National Assembly, and draft executive decrees to the President; 

3.  To issue decrees and decisions related to governance, economic and social affairs, science, 

technology, natural resources, environment, national defense and security, and foreign affairs; 

4.  To determine visions, strategic plans, socio-economic development plans, and state budgets, and 

propose them to the National Assembly for review and approval; 

5.  To propose to the National Assembly the establishment, merger, or dissolution of ministries, 
ministry-level organizations, the capital city, and provinces, as well as adjustments to their 
boundaries; 

6.  To decide on the establishment, merger, or dissolution of departments or ministry-equivalent 

organizations; 

7.  To decide on the establishment, merger, or dissolution of districts, municipalities, and their 

boundaries after approval by provincial People’s Assemblies; 

8.  To establish or dissolve special economic zones, specific economic zones, and international 

border checkpoints. 

9.  To lead, monitor, and inspect the operations of various bodies, including local administrative 

bodies, and national defense and security forces; 

10.  To decide on granting nationality, revoking nationality, restoring nationality, or reinstating Lao 

nationality; 

11.  To confer honorary citizenship to foreigners; 

17 



 12.  To decide on signing, approving, acceding to, or terminating international treaties and agreements 

on behalf of the Lao People’s Democratic Republic; 

13.  To suspend, repeal, or annul decisions, orders, or guidelines issued by ministries, 

ministry-equivalent bodies, and local administrations that contradict laws and other legal 
instruments, except for judgments and decisions of the People’s Court and the Office of the 
Public Prosecutor. 

14.  To report on its activities to the National Assembly, the Standing Committee of the National 

Assembly, and the President.

(Amended) 

The Provincial People’s Assembly has the following rights and duties: 

1.  To deliberate and approve resolutions that have general binding effect within its locality; 

2.  To deliberate and approve the local socio-economic development plan, state budget plan, and any 

proposed amendments to the local budget; 

3.  To elect or dismiss the President, Vice President, and members of the Standing Committee of the 

Provincial People’s Assembly; 

4.  To deliberate and approve the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of Administrative Committee 

Chairpersons and the Secretary General of the Provincial People’s Assembly; 

5.  To deliberate and approve the organizational structure of the Provincial People’s Assembly and 

any creation, integration, or dissolution of its departments; 

6.  To deliberate and approve the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of the Administrative 

Committee Chairpersons of the Capital or Province; 

19 



 7.  To deliberate and approve the organizational structure of the Provincial or Capital Administrative 

Committees; and proposals concerning the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of Vice 
Chairpersons of the Capital/Provincial Committees, Heads of Departments, Heads of 
department-equivalent bodies within the Capital/Provinces, Chairpersons of District/Municipality 
Administrative Committees. 

8.  To deliberate and approve the proposal of the Chairpersons of the Provincial or Capital 

Administrative Committees concerning the establishment, merging, or dissolution of departments 
and department-equivalent offices of the Capital or Province; 

9.  To deliberate and approve the proposal for the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of the President 

of the People’s Court at the Capital, Province, and District-levels; 

10.  To deliberate and approve the proposal for the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of the Head of 

the People’s Prosecutor’s Office at the Capital, Province, and District-levels; 

11.  To deliberate and approve the creation, dissolution, or boundary modification of districts, cities, 

and sub-districts; 

12.  To monitor and inspect the compliance and implementation of the Constitution and laws by local 

state organizations; 

13.  To revoke or annul regulations, orders, recommendations, or other legal acts of any agency under 
its jurisdiction or subordinate levels that conflict with the law, except for court judgments or 
decisions of the People’s Prosecutor’s Office.

(Amended) 

The Standing Committee of the Provincial People’s Assembly has the following rights and duties: 

1.  To prepare and convene sessions of the Provincial People’s Assembly; 

2.  To deliberate and approve proposals for the appointment, transfer, or dismissal of People’s Court 

officials, as specified by law; 

20 



 3.  To deliberate and approve the proposal for the appointment of the members of the Provincial, 

Capital, or District/City Prosecutor’s Committee; 

4.  To appoint the local election committee; 

5.  To receive and consider citizen petitions for justice within its jurisdiction.

(Amended) 

Local administrative bodies shall have the following rights and duties: 

1.  Implement the Constitution, laws, legislation, resolutions of the local people’s assembly, and local 

socio-economic development and budget plans; 

2.  Develop local socio-economic development plans, budget plans, and national defense–security 

plans for submission to the local people’s assembly for consideration and approval; 

3.  Manage and oversee political, economic, cultural-social, national defense–security, natural 

resources, environmental, and other affairs in their jurisdictions; 

4.  Issue regulations related to economic, social, and defense-security management; 

5.  Monitor and inspect the implementation of duties by agencies under their supervision; 

22 





 6.  Manage Lao citizens, foreigners, stateless persons, and foreign residents within their territories; 

7.  Receive, review, and resolve petitions and proposals of the people; 

8.  Engage in foreign relations and cooperation as assigned by the Government; 

9.  Summarize and report on the overall implementation of duties in their jurisdictions to higher-level 

authorities and to their local people’s assemblies on a regular basis. 

CHAPTER X 

People’s Courts and People’s Prosecutor

(New) 

The State Inspection Authority has the rights and duties as follows: 

1.  To inspect the implementation of policies, laws, plans, projects, duties, rights, and responsibilities 

of organizations that use state assets and budgets; 

2.  To monitor the conduct and responsibilities of civil servants, military, police, state enterprise 

employees, and other individuals who violate the law; 

3.  To inspect the implementation of inspection findings and court verdicts in corruption and money 

laundering cases related to corruption; 

4.  To audit state investment projects from the start, conduct inspections following audits and 

resolutions of the National Assembly or local People’s Assemblies, and handle asset declaration 
and income reporting; 

5.  To examine and address complaints, to prevent and combat corruption, and to investigate cases of 

corruption and related money laundering; 

6.  To propose corrective measures and solutions based on inspection findings; 

7.  To report on operations, especially inspection outcomes and corrective actions, to the National 

Assembly or local People’s Assemblies.
